ching for\n314-  `fs` directly leaves no trace in the array.\n315-\n316-## Testing `src/renderer/main.js`\n317-\n318-`main.js` cannot be required in a unit test at all -- not even with\n319-the compile-it-yourself recipe above. Its module body calls\n320-`State.load()`, and it pulls in React, ReactDOM, every page and the\n321-whole controller graph. `test/unit/main-ipc-call-sites.js` therefore\n322-checks it statically, but goes one step past a channel-string ban: it\n323-extracts every `ipcOs.x` / `ipcEngine.x` member the file reaches for\n324-and resolves each against the `export function` names in the shim\n325-source, so a typo'd or invented export fails there instead of\n326-silently becoming `undefined` at runtime. It also asserts the set of\n327-shim exports covers every channel `main.js` spoke before the\n328-migration, which is the only automated guard against a channel being","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"11f41803-e0fe-4b16-bc79-29d66fb80fef","uuid":"4ca2a690-f71b-4738-b3a2-fdc39df1593f","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:10:01.048Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"288:  The same collision used to bite two files that both required the\n289-  main process's `ipc` module; both those files went with the host in\n290-  US-072. The recipe they used is still the one to reach for when two\n291-  files need different stubs of the same built module:\n292-  `delete require.cache[...]` before requiring, then put the previous\n293-  entry back, so both instances keep the stubs they were built with.\n294-- **Stub every built module the controller graph reaches, not just the\n295-  one under test.** Letting a real shim module load here binds *this*\n296-  file's host stub into it for the whole run, which breaks\n297-  `platform-clipboard.js` / `platform-ipc-engine.js` later in the\n298-  glob. `playback-controller` reaches `lib/state` -> `lib/migrations`\n299-  that way, which is exactly what `migrations.js` tests against.\n300-- **`migrations.js` nev